* Roadmap

- This lesson covers **overfitting**
  - What it is
  - Why it happens
  - How to recognize it through concrete regression and classification examples
- It then derives the **bias-variance decomposition**, showing how
  out-of-sample error splits into bias, variance, and noise as a function of
  model complexity
  - It separates deterministic from stochastic noise
  - It gives a rule of thumb to match model complexity to the amount of data
- It closes with **learning curves**, the dual view of bias-variance analysis
  as a function of training set size rather than model complexity

# Overfitting

* Overfitting: Definition

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @Definition@: **Overfitting** occurs when the model fits the data more than what is
  warranted `#cite("abumostafa2012learning")`{=typst}
  - Surpass point where $E_{out}$ is minimal (optimal fit)
  - Model complexity too high for data/noise

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```tikz
\bfseries
% Axis
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (7,0) node[right] {$\text{VC dimension, } d_{\text{vc}}$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5) node[above] {Error};

% Dashed line for optimal VC dimension
\draw[dashed, thick] (2.8,0) -- (2.8,4.5);
\node at (2.8,-0.3) {$d_{\text{vc}}^*$};

% In-sample error curve
\draw[thick, blue] plot[smooth, domain=0.6:6] (\x, {2.0/(0.5*\x^2.0+0.3)});

% Out-of-sample error curve
\draw[thick, red] plot[smooth, domain=0.6:6] (\x, {2.0/(0.5*\x^2.0+0.3) + 1.0*(\x/1.5)^0.6});

% Labels
\node[blue] at (5.0,0.7) {In-sample Error};
\node[red] at (6.0,2.8) {Out-of-sample Error};
```

::::
:::

- @Fact@: **Fitting noise** instead of signal is _not useless but actually harmful_
  - Model infers in-sample pattern that, when extrapolated out-of-sample,
    deviates from target function $\implies$ poor generalization

* Optimal Fit

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @Remark@: The opposite of overfitting is not underfitting, but **optimal fit**,
  i.e., train a model with proper complexity for the data

- @Definition@: the **generalization error** is the error $E_{out} - E_{in}$ when
  going from in-sample to out-of-sample
  - The degradation of fit, i.e., the relative generalization error, is
    $\frac{E_{out} - E_{in}}{E_{out}}$

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Optimal_fit.png)

::::
:::

- @Characteristics@: the optimal fit
  - Implies $E_{out}$ is minimal, not $E_{in}$
  - Does not imply generalization error $E_{out} - E_{in}$ is minimal

* Overfitting: Regression Example
- @Problem@: predict diamond price as a function of carat size (regression
  problem)

- @True relationship@ is
  $$
  Price = c \cdot Carat^2 + \varepsilon
  $$
  where:
  - Square function: larger, rarer diamonds command a price premium
  - Noise $\varepsilon$: e.g., missing features, true stochasticity

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}

- @Fitting data with:@
  - _Line_ (underfit)
    - High bias (large error)
    - Low variance (stable model)
  - _Polynomial of degree 2_ (right fit)
  - _Polynomial of degree 10_ (overfit / `
    - Low bias
    - High variance (Wiggly curve, too many degrees of freedom)
::::
:::: {.column width=35%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Diamond_price_underfit.png){width=30%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Diamond_price_rightfit.png){width=30%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Diamond_price_overfit.png){width=30%}

::::
:::

* Overfitting: Classification Example
- @Problem@: separate 2 classes using 2 features $x_1, x_2$
  - The true class boundary has a parabola shape

- @Variants@: use logistic regression with a decision boundary
  $\vw^T \phi(\vx) = 0$ for different features $\phi(\vx)$
  - _Line_: $\phi = (1, x_1, x_2)$
    - Underfit
    - High bias, low variance
  - _Quadratic_: $\phi = (1, x_1, x_2, x_1^2, x_1 x_2, x_2^2)$
    - Right fit
  - _Polynomial of degree 10_: all monomials $x_1^i x_2^j$ with $i + j \le 10$
    - Overfit (wiggly decision boundary)
    - Low bias, high variance

