# Bias-Variance Analysis

* Hypothesis Set and Bias-Variance Analysis
- @Learning@ consists in finding $g \in \calH$ such that $g \approx f$ where
  $f$ is an unknown function

- @Key idea@: the **tradeoff in learning** is between low-complexity and
  high-complexity models, each with a typical failure mode
  `#cite("geman1992biasvariance")`{=typst}

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Low complexity model", "High complexity model"),
  rows: (
    ([High bias], [High variance]),
    ([Underfitting], [Overfitting]),
    ([Less complex $cal(H)$ / $h$], [More complex $cal(H)$ / $h$]),
    ([Poor approximation (large $E_"in"$)],
     [Poor generalization (large $E_"out" - E_"in"$)]),
  ),
  bold-first-col: false,
)
```

* VC Analysis vs Bias-Variance Analysis

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}

- @Comparison@: both VC analysis and bias-variance analysis are concerned with
  the hypothesis set $\calH$

- @VC analysis@ `#cite("vapnikchervonenkis1971uniform")`{=typst}:
  $$
  E_{out} \le E_{in} + \Omega(\calH)
  $$

- @Bias-variance analysis@
  $$
  E_{out} = \text{bias}^2 + \text{variance}
  $$

- @Remark@: both plots show the expected error as a function of the training
  set size $N$
  - These are _learning curves_, discussed at the end of this lesson

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}

