## Deriving the Bias-Variance Decomposition

* Decomposing Error in Bias-Variance (1/4)
- @Setup@
  - Regression set-up: target is a real-valued function $f(\vx)$
  - Hypothesis set: $\calH = \{ h_1(\vx), h_2(\vx), ... h_n(\vx) \}$
  - Training data: $\calD$ with $N$ examples
  - Squared error: $E_{out} = \EE[(g(\vx) - f(\vx))^2]$

- @Goal@
  - Choose the best function $g \in \calH$ that approximates unknown $f$ out of
    sample

- @Question@
  - What is the out-of-sample error $E_{out}(g)$ as function of $\calH$ for a
    training set of $N$ examples?

* Decomposing Error in Bias-Variance (2/4)
- @Step 1@: the final hypothesis $g$ depends on training set $D$, so make the
  dependency explicit $g^{(D)}$:
  $$
  E_{out}(g^{(D)})
  \triangleq \EE_{\vx}[ ( g^{(D)}(\vx) - f(\vx)) ^ 2 ]
  $$

- @Goal@: we are interested in
  - Hypothesis set $\calH$ rather than specific $h$
  - Training set of $N$ examples, rather than a specific $D$

- @Step 2@: remove dependency from $D$ by averaging over all possible training
  sets $D$ with $N$ examples:
  $$
  E_{out}(\calH)
  \triangleq \EE_{D}[ E_{out}(g^{(D)}) ]
  = \EE_{D}[\EE_{\vx}[ ( g^{(D)}(\vx) - f(\vx)) ^ 2 ]]
  $$

* Decomposing Error in Bias-Variance (3/4)
- @Step 3@: switch the order of the expectations since the quantity is
  non-negative:
  $$
  E_{out}(\calH)
  = \EE_{\vx}[ \EE_{D}[ ( g^{(D)}(\vx) - f(\vx)) ^ 2 ] ]
  $$

- @Step 4@: focus on $\EE_D [( g^{(D)}(\vx) - f(\vx) ) ^ 2 ]$ which is a
  function of $\vx$

- @Definition@: the **average hypothesis** over all training sets is:
  $$
  \overline{g}(\vx) \triangleq \EE_D [ g^{(D)} (\vx) ]
  $$

- @Step 5@: add and subtract it inside the $\EE_D$ expression:

  \begingroup \small
  $$
  \begin{aligned}
  E_{out}(\calH)
  = & \EE_{\vx} \left[ \EE_D \left[ \left(
  g^{(D)}(\vx) - f(\vx)
  \right) ^ 2 \right] \right]\\
  %
  = & \EE_{\vx} \EE_D [ (
  g^{(D)} - \overline{g} +
  \overline{g} - f
  ) ^ 2 ]\\
  = & \EE_{\vx} \EE_D [
  (g^{(D)} - \overline{g}) ^ 2 +
  (\overline{g} - f ) ^ 2 +
  2 (g^{(D)} - \overline{g}) (\overline{g} - f)
  ]\\
  %
  & (\EE_D \text{ is linear and } (\overline{g} - f )
  \text{ doesn't depend on } D) \\
  %
  = & \EE_{\vx} \left[
  \EE_D[(g^{(D)} - \overline{g}) ^ 2] +
  (\overline{g} - f ) ^ 2 +
  2 \EE_D[(g^{(D)} - \overline{g})] (\overline{g} - f)
  \right]\\
  \end{aligned}
  $$
  \endgroup

* Decomposing Error in Bias-Variance (4/4)
- @Step 6@: the cross term
  $$
  2 \EE_D[(g^{(D)} - \overline{g})] (\overline{g} - f)
  $$
  disappears, since $\overline{g}$ is the mean of $g^{(D)}$ over $D$:
  $$
  \EE_D[g^{(D)} - \overline{g}]
  = \EE_D[g^{(D)}] - \overline{g}
  = \overline{g} - \overline{g}
  = 0
  $$
  and $(\overline{g} - f)$ does not depend on $D$, so the cross term is
  $2 \cdot 0 \cdot (\overline{g} - f) = 0$

- @Result@: finally
  $$
  \begin{alignat*}{3}
  E_{out}(\calH)
  & = \EE_{\vx} [ \EE_D [ ( g^{(D)} - \overline{g} ) ^ 2 ] +
  ( \overline{g}(\vx) - f(\vx) ) ^ 2 ]
  &
  \\
  & = \EE_{\vx} [ \EE_D [ ( g^{(D)} - \overline{g} ) ^ 2 ] ] +
  \EE_{\vx}[( \overline{g} - f) ^ 2]
  & (\EE_{\vx} \text{ is linear}) \\
  & = \EE_{\vx} [ \text{var}(\vx) ] + \EE_{\vx} [ \text{bias}(\vx)^2 ]
  &
  \\
  & = \text{variance} + \text{bias}^2
  &
  \\
  \end{alignat*}
  $$
  where $\text{var}(\vx) \triangleq \EE_D[(g^{(D)}(\vx) - \overline{g}(\vx))^2]$
  and $\text{bias}(\vx) \triangleq \overline{g}(\vx) - f(\vx)$

// TODO: Add a numerical example

* Interpretation of Average Hypothesis
- @Definition@: The **average hypothesis** over all training sets is:
  $$
  \overline{g}(\vx) \triangleq \EE_D [ g^{(D)} (\vx) ]
  $$

- @Intuition@: It can be interpreted as the "best" hypothesis from $\calH$ training
  on $N$ samples
  - Note: $\overline{g}$ is not necessarily $\in \calH$, so it can differ from
    the best hypothesis $h^* \in \calH$

- @Remark@: it is like **ensemble learning**
  - Consider all the possible data sets $D$ with $N$ samples
  - Learn $g$ from each $D$
  - Average all the learned hypotheses

* Interpretation of Bias Terms
::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}

- @Key idea@: the out-of-sample error can be decomposed as:
  $$
  E_{out}(\calH) = \text{bias}^2 + \text{variance}
  $$

- @Bias term@
  $$
  \text{bias}^2 \triangleq \EE_{\vx} [ ( \overline{g}(\vx) - f(\vx) ) ^ 2 ]
  $$
  - Does not depend on a specific data set $D$, as $\overline{g}$ averages over
    all of them
    - It still depends on the learning algorithm and on $N$
  - Measures how limited $\calH$ is
    - I.e., the ability of $\calH$ to approximate the target with infinite
      training sets

::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.bias_variance.png)

::::
:::

* Interpretation of Variance Term
::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}

- @Variance term@
  $$
  \text{variance} \triangleq
  \EE_{\vx} \EE_D [ ( g^{(D)}(\vx) - \overline{g}(\vx) ) ^ 2]
  $$
  - Measures variability of the learned hypothesis from $D$ for any $\vx$
    - With infinite training sets, we could focus on the "best" $g$, which is
      $\overline{g}$
    - But we have only one data set $D$ at a time, incurring a cost

::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.bias_variance_decomposition.png)

::::
:::

* Variance and Bias Term Varying Cardinality of $\calH$

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}

- @Single function@: the hypothesis set has one function
  $$
  \calH = \{ h \ne f \}
  $$
  - Large bias
    - $h$ might be far from $f$
  - Variance = 0
    - No cost in choosing hypothesis
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Bias_Variance_tradeoff_example1.png)

::::
:::

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}

\vspace{1cm}

- @Many functions@: the hypothesis set has many functions
  $$
  \calH = \{ h_1, h_2, \ldots, h_M \}
  $$
  - Bias can be 0
    - E.g., if $f \in \calH$
  - Large variance
    - Depending on data set $D$, end up far from $f$
    - Larger $\calH$, farther $g$ from $f$
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Bias_Variance_tradeoff_example2.png)

::::
:::

