## Bias-Variance Tradeoff in Practice

* Bias-Variance Trade-Off: Numerical Example (1/3)

- @Problem@:
  - Target function $f(x) = \sin(\pi x), x \in [-1, 1]$
  - Noiseless target
  - You have $f(\vx)$ for $N = 2$ points

- @Two hypotheses sets@ $\calH$:
  - Constant model: $\calH_0: h(x) = b$
  - Linear model: $\calH_1: h(x) = ax + b$

- @Which model is best@?
  - Depends on what is the goal!
  - Best for _approximation_: minimal error approximating the sinusoid
  - Best for _learning_: learn the unknown function with minimal error from 2 points

* Bias-Variance Trade-Off: Numerical Example (2/3)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}

- @Approximation@
  - $E_{out}(g_0) = 0.5$
    - $g_0$ is a constant and approximates the sinusoid poorly (higher bias)
  - $E_{out}(g_1) \approx 0.2$
    - $g_1$ is a line and has more degrees of freedom (lower bias)
  - The line model _approximates better_ than the constant model

::::
:::: {.column width=30%}

// TODO(gp): Regenerate these two figures, missing on disk.
//![Constant model fit on one training set](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Learning_Once_0.png)
//![Linear model fit on one training set](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Learning_Once_1.png)

::::
:::

* Bias-Variance Trade-Off: Numerical Example (3/3)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- @Learning@
  - Algorithm:
    - Pick $N$ points as training set $D$
    - Learn $g^{(D)}$ from $D$
    - Compute $E_{out}(g^{(D)})$
  - Average over all data sets $D$:
    $$
    \EE_D [ E_{out}(g^{(D)}) ] = \text{bias}^2 + \text{variance}
    $$
  - $E_{out}(g_0) = 0.5 + 0.25 = 0.75$
    - $g_0$ is more stable given the data set (lower variance)
  - $E_{out}(g_1) = 0.21 + 1.69 = 1.90$
    - $g_1$ heavily depends on the training set (higher variance)
  - The constant model _learns better_ than the line model

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Bias_Variance_Numerical_Example1.png)

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Bias_Variance_Numerical_Example2.png)

::::
:::

* How to Measure the Model Complexity
- @Techniques@: the model complexity is measured by
  - Number of features
  - Parameters for model form / degrees of freedom, e.g.,
    - VC dimension $d_{VC}$
    - Degree of polynomials
    - $k$ in KNN (smaller $k$ means a more complex model)
    - $\nu$ in NuSVM
  - Regularization param $\lambda$
  - Training epochs for neural network

* Bias-Variance Curves
- @Definition@: **bias-variance curves** are plots of $E_{in}$ and $E_{out}$ as
  the complexity of the model increases `#cite("hastie2009elements")`{=typst}

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Characteristics@: typical shape of bias-variance curves
  - $E_{in}$ and $E_{out}$ start close together
  - $E_{in}$ decreases with increasing model complexity and can even go to 0
  - $E_{out}$ is always larger than $E_{in}$
  - $E_{out}$ is the sum of $\text{bias}^2$ and variance
  - $E_{out}$ has a bowl shape and reaches a minimum for optimal fit
  - Before the minimum there is a "high bias / underfitting" regime
  - After the minimum there is a "high variance / overfitting" regime
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.bias_variance_curve.png)

::::
:::

* Double Descent

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}

- @Remark@: the bowl shape of $E_{out}$ describes classical models
  `#cite("belkin2019reconciling")`{=typst}

- @Characteristics@: double descent in over-parameterized models (e.g., deep
  networks)
  - $E_{in}$ reaches 0 at the interpolation threshold
  - $E_{out}$ peaks near the threshold
  - Past the threshold $E_{out}$ can decrease again as complexity grows

- @Interpretation@: the classical regime of bias-variance analysis still holds
  - It does not describe what happens past the interpolation threshold

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

