\draw[thick, blue] (4.2,0) -- (7.6,0);

% E_out curve
\draw[thick, red] plot[smooth] coordinates {(0.4,3.0) (1.3,1.9) (2.2,1.5) (3.0,1.9) (3.7,2.9) (4.2,3.8) (4.7,3.0) (5.5,2.0) (6.5,1.5) (7.6,1.3)};

% Labels
\node[blue] at (6.2,0.4) {$E_{\text{in}}$};
\node[red] at (6.4,2.2) {$E_{\text{out}}$};
\node[gray, font=\small] at (1.8,3.7) {Classical};
\node[gray, font=\small] at (6.2,3.7) {Modern};
```

::::
:::

* Bias-Variance Curves and Regularization

- @Definition@: **regularization** adds a complexity penalty to the in-sample
  error
  $$
  E_{aug}(\vw) \triangleq E_{in}(\vw) + \frac{\lambda}{N} \Omega(\vw)
  $$
  - E.g., weight decay uses $\Omega(\vw) = \vw^T \vw$

- @Method@: learn the optimal model $\vw(\lambda)$ for each value of
  $\lambda = \{..., 10^{-1}, 1.0, 10, ...\}$:
  $$
  \vw(\lambda) = \argmin_{\vw} E_{aug}(\vw)
  $$
  - Pick $\lambda$ using held-out data, not $E_{in}$

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @Interpretation@: the regularization parameter $\lambda$ sets the complexity
  - Small $\lambda$: complex model, low bias, high variance
  - Large $\lambda$: simple model, high bias, low variance
  - An intermediate $\lambda$ is optimal

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

