::::
:::

* High-Bias vs High-Variance Regime
::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Remark@: from the learning curve you can see the two regimes

- @High-variance regime@ for small $N$
  - $E_{in}$ is small
  - Small data set $D$ $\implies$ high dependency on $D$
  - More data helps reduce gap between $E_{in}$ and $E_{out}$

- @High-bias regime@ for large $N$
  - $E_{in}$ is large; flattens for large $N$
  - Best model fitted; more data won't help
  - The gap $E_{out} - E_{in}$ closes as $N$ grows
::::
:::: {.column width=30%}

// TODO(gp): Convert to tikz and comment out the pics in place.
![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Irreducible_Error1.png)

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Irreducible_Error2.png)

::::
:::

* Diagnosing with Learning Curves

- @Goal@: use the learning curves of a fixed model to decide whether more data
  helps

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @High-bias model@
  - $E_{in}$ and $E_{out}$ converge quickly to a high plateau
  - Small gap: the model is too simple
  - More data does not help: increase the model complexity

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @High-variance model@
  - $E_{in}$ stays low and the gap to $E_{out}$ is large
  - The gap closes slowly as $N$ grows
  - More data helps: also try regularization or a simpler model

::::
:::

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

```tikz
\bfseries
% Axes
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (7,0) node[below] {$N$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,4) node[above] {Error};
\node at (3.5,3.9) {High Bias};

% E_in curve
\draw[thick, blue] plot[smooth, domain=0.4:6.5] (\x, {2.2 - 1.3/(0.9*\x + 0.45)});

% E_out curve
\draw[thick, red] plot[smooth, domain=0.4:6.5] (\x, {2.2 + 1.0/(0.9*\x + 0.45)});

% Labels
\node[blue] at (5.8,1.7) {$E_{\text{in}}$};
\node[red] at (5.8,2.7) {$E_{\text{out}}$};
```

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```tikz
\bfseries
% Axes
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (7,0) node[below] {$N$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,4) node[above] {Error};
\node at (3.5,3.9) {High Variance};

% E_in curve
\draw[thick, blue] plot[smooth, domain=0.4:6.5] (\x, {0.8 - 0.6/(0.6*\x + 0.9)});

% E_out curve
\draw[thick, red] plot[smooth, domain=0.4:6.5] (\x, {0.8 + 2.0/(0.45*\x + 0.55)});

% Labels
\node[blue] at (5.8,0.35) {$E_{\text{in}}$};
\node[red] at (5.8,1.85) {$E_{\text{out}}$};
```

::::
:::

* Learning Curves for Linear Regression
- @Problem@: noisy linear target $y = \vw^T \vx + \varepsilon$ with noise
  variance $\sigma^2$, fit by least squares with $d + 1$ parameters on $N$
  points `#cite("abumostafa2012learning")`{=typst}

- @Result@: for $N > d + 1$ the expected errors are
  $$
  \begin{aligned}
  \EE_D[E_{in}] & = \sigma^2 \left( 1 - \frac{d + 1}{N} \right) \\
  \EE_D[E_{out}] & = \sigma^2 \left( 1 + \frac{d + 1}{N} \right)
  \end{aligned}
  $$
  - For $N \le d + 1$ the model fits signal and noise perfectly: $E_{in} = 0$

- @Interpretation@
  - $E_{in}$ increases and $E_{out}$ decreases towards the same limit
    $\sigma^2$ as $N$ grows
  - The generalization error is $E_{out} - E_{in} = 2 \sigma^2 \frac{d + 1}{N}$
  - What matters is the ratio $\frac{d + 1}{N}$, in line with the rule of thumb
    $N \ge 10 \, d_{VC}$

// Derivation of the results above (not shown)
//* Example of learning curves for linear regression
//- Consider a noisy target
//
//  $$
//  y = \vw^T \vx + \varepsilon
//  $$
//
//- The optimal estimate of $\vw$ is given by:
//
//  $$
//  \hat{\vw} = (\mX^T \mX)^{-1} \mX^T \vy
//  $$
//
//  where the measured $\vy = \mX \vw + \vvarepsilon$, so
//
//  $$
//  \begin{aligned}
//  \hat{\vw}
//  &= (\mX^T \mX)^{-1} \mX^T (\mX \vw + \vvarepsilon) \\
//  &= (\mX^T \mX)^{-1} \mX^T \mX \vw +
//  (\mX^T \mX)^{-1} \mX^T \vvarepsilon \\
//  &= \vw + (\mX^T \mX)^{-1} \mX^T \vvarepsilon \\
//  \end{aligned}
//  $$
//
//- We can compute in-sample error by replacing the measured $\vy$ and the
//  estimated $\vw$:
//
//  $$
//  \begin{aligned}
//  E_{in}
//  &= \|\vy - \mX \hat{\vw} \|^2 \\
//  &= \| (\mX \vw + \vvarepsilon) -
//  (\mX \vw +
//  \mX (\mX^T \mX)^{-1} \mX^T \vvarepsilon) \\
//  &= \| \vvarepsilon -
//  \mX (\mX^T \mX)^{-1} \mX^T \vvarepsilon) \\
//  &= \| (\mI - \mX (\mX^T \mX)^{-1} \mX^T) \vvarepsilon) \\
//  \end{aligned}
//  $$
//
//- Out-sample error: $E_{out} = \|\vy' - \mX \hat{\vw}\|^2$ using a different
//  realization of the noise
//
//- For $N \le d + 1$, $E_{in} = 0$ since we have $d + 1$ degrees of freedom and
//  we can capture signal and noise perfectly
//- Then $E_{in}$ increases and goes towards $\sigma ^ 2$ which is related to the
//  noise since we cannot capture it with out hypothesis set
//
//- For linear regression we can compute analytically the solution
//  $$
//  \begin{aligned}
//  & E_{in} = \sigma^2 (1 - \frac{d + 1}{N}) \\
//  & E_{out} = \sigma^2 (1 + \frac{d + 1}{N}) \\
//  \end{aligned}
//  $$
//- TODO: Why these relationships?
//- The generalization error is: $E_{out} - E_{in} = 2 \sigma^2 (\frac{d + 1}{N})$
//  which shows that what matters is $\frac{d + 1}{N}$ (our rule of thumb was
//  $N > 10 d_{VC}$)

* Summary

- @Key idea@: overfitting happens when a model fits noise instead of signal,
  and the bias-variance decomposition explains why: $E_{out} = \text{bias}^2 +
  \text{variance}$ (plus noise for a noisy target)

- @Recap@
  - _Overfitting_: going past the model complexity that minimizes $E_{out}$
    gains in-sample fit at the cost of out-of-sample error
  - _Bias-variance decomposition_: $E_{out}$ splits into bias (how well
    $\calH$ can approximate $f$ on average) and variance (how much the
    learned $g$ varies across training sets)
  - _Noise_: deterministic noise (bias) and stochastic noise ($\sigma^2$) both
    push a model to overfit, and the training set cannot tell them apart
  - _Complexity control_: regularization and other complexity knobs trade
    bias for variance; the rule of thumb $N \ge 10 \, d_{VC}$ matches model
    complexity to available data
  - _Learning curves_: the dual view of bias-variance analysis, showing
    $E_{in}$ and $E_{out}$ converge to the same value as the training set size
    $N$ grows, and whether more data helps

